Q: EFCore.BulkExtensions 10: how to update only selected properties of an Owned entity? A: <p>This is a <strong>known regression / issue</strong> introduced during the internal refactoring of column mapping and metadata processing in newer major versions (v8+ through v10) of <code>EFCore.BulkExtensions</code>.</p>
<h3>What Changed in Version 10?</h3>
<ol>
<li><p><strong><code>PropertiesToInclude</code> throwing <code>MultiplePropertyListSetException</code>:</strong> In <code>EFCore.BulkExtensions</code> 10.x, properties filtering was separated explicitly based on operation type. Setting <code>PropertiesToInclude</code> on a <code>BulkUpdate</code> is treated as a configuration conflict because <code>PropertiesToIncludeOnUpdate</code> is now the dedicated property list for update operations.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>PropertiesToIncludeOnUpdate</code> updating all columns of the Owned Type:</strong> When metadata mapping was refactored in EF Core 8/9/10, column resolution for complex navigation properties (owned types) broke when generating the <code>MERGE</code> or <code>UPDATE</code> SQL statement. The library resolves the top-level navigation property (<code>OwnedData</code>), fails to map individual shadow/flattened properties like <code>OwnedData.Property1</code> against its updated column dictionary, and falls back to including the entire owned type in the SQL <code>SET</code> clause.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3>Workarounds</h3>
<p>Until a fix is released for this issue, you can use the following alternatives to update specific properties of an owned entity:</p>
<h4>Workaround 1: Use Native EF Core <code>ExecuteUpdateAsync</code> (Recommended)</h4>
<p>If you don't specifically need to stage an in-memory <code>List&lt;MainEntity&gt;</code> via temporary tables, EF Core 7+ natively supports set-based updates via <code>ExecuteUpdateAsync</code>. This bypasses third-party libraries and generates clean, targeted SQL:</p>
<p>C#</p>
<pre><code>// Updates only specific properties of the owned type directly in SQL
await context.MainEntities
    .Where(x =&gt; idList.Contains(x.Id)) // or your filter
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =&gt; s
        .SetProperty(x =&gt; x.OwnedData.Property1, x =&gt; newPropertyValue1)
        .SetProperty(x =&gt; x.OwnedData.Property2, x =&gt; newPropertyValue2)
    );
</code></pre>
<h4>Workaround 2: Explicit Column Names via <code>ColumnInputExpression</code></h4>
<p>In some versions of <code>EFCore.BulkExtensions</code>, targeting flattened DB column names directly via <code>ColumnInputExpression</code> bypasses the broken navigation-property string parser:</p>
<p>C#</p>
<pre><code>var bulkConfig = new BulkConfig
{
    UseTempDB = true,
    IgnoreRowVersion = true,
    UnderlyingTransaction = (x) =&gt; db.GetDbTransaction(),

    // Explicitly select the database columns for the owned type
    ColumnInputExpression = x =&gt; new 
    { 
        x.OwnedData.Property1, 
        x.OwnedData.Property2, 
        x.OwnedData.Property3 
    }
};

await context.BulkUpdateAsync(entities, bulkConfig);
</code></pre>
<h4>Workaround 3: Flattened Shadow Property Names</h4>
<p>If dot-notation navigation strings (<code>&quot;OwnedData.Property1&quot;</code>) are ignored, pass the actual SQL table column name / flattened shadow property name used by EF Core (typically <code>OwnedData_Property1</code>):</p>
<p>C#</p>
<pre><code>var bulkConfig = new BulkConfig
{
    UseTempDB = true,
    IgnoreRowVersion = true,
    PropertiesToIncludeOnUpdate = new List&lt;string&gt;
    {
        &quot;OwnedData_Property1&quot;,
        &quot;OwnedData_Property2&quot;,
        &quot;OwnedData_Property3&quot;
    }
};

await context.BulkUpdateAsync(entities, bulkConfig);
</code></pre>

Q: Could this fetch response still be cached by the browser? A: <p>With <code>cache: &quot;no-store&quot;</code> repeating that fetch() should make a new network request rather than reuse the browsers HTTP cache.</p>
<p>The blob URL is separate. createObjectURL() creates a temporary URL for the Blob and revokeObjectURL() removes it. It does not clear or affect the HTTP cache.</p>
<p>A faster second request does not necessarily mean the browser caches it. The connection may already be open or the server or OS may be serving the file faster the second time. Check the network tab. You should see a new request and it should not say memory cache or disk cache.</p>
<p>Revoking the object URL also not guarantee the memory is released immediately if something still holds a reference to the Blob.</p>
<pre><code>
audio.pause();
audio.removeAttribute(&quot;src&quot;);
audio.load();

URL.revokeObjectURL(objectURL);
objectURL = null;
blob = null;
</code></pre>

Q: What is the difference between the === and == comparators? A: <p>In PHP and JavaScript (I'm not sure of other languages where the triple <code>===</code> syntax is valid) the difference is that <code>===</code> is a strict comparison. While <code>==</code> is loose. That means that <code>===</code> compares value and type, but <code>==</code> just compares value. A perfect example of this is the buggy PHP code below:</p>

<pre><code>$str = 'Zebraman stole my child\'s pet lime!';

// Search for zebra man
if(strpos($str, 'Zebraman')){
    echo 'The string contains "Zebraman"';
}else{
    echo 'The string doesn\'t contain "Zebraman"';
}
</code></pre>

<p><a href="http://codepad.viper-7.com/1B850u" rel="nofollow"><strong>Example Here</strong></a></p>

<p>Since <code>strpos($str, 'Zebraman')</code> returns <code>0</code> (The index of the string <code>Zebraman</code>), and since <code>0</code> is <code>falsy</code>. That code will output <code>The string doesn't contain "Zebraman"</code>. The correct code uses a strict comparison with false:</p>

<pre><code>$str = 'Zebraman stole my child\'s pet lime!';

// Search for zebra man
if(strpos($str, 'Zebraman') !== false){
    echo 'The string contains "Zebraman"';
}else{
    echo 'The string doesn\'t contain "Zebraman"';
}
</code></pre>

<p><a href="http://codepad.viper-7.com/rwjnFN" rel="nofollow"><strong>Example Here</strong></a></p>

<p><a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.strpos.php" rel="nofollow">See the PHP man page on strpos</a></p>
